  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts a chloroplast and a mitochondrion and illustrates their complementary roles in cellular energy metabolism. On the left, light is shown entering the chloroplast, which is drawn in green with stacked internal membranes and labeled cpDNA. This organelle appears to use light energy to produce oxygen and a sugar, indicated by the arrow pointing toward the mitochondrion with the formula for glucose plus O2. On the right, the mitochondrion is shown in gray-blue with folded inner membranes and labeled mtDNA. A second arrow shows the reverse flow, with CO2 and H2O returning toward the chloroplast, while another arrow leaving the mitochondrion indicates ATP production.\n\nTaken together, the image summarizes the link between photosynthesis and cellular respiration: chloroplasts convert light into chemical energy stored in sugars and release oxygen, and mitochondria consume those products to generate ATP, releasing carbon dioxide and water. The inclusion of cpDNA and mtDNA emphasizes that both organelles contain their own DNA. The main visual message is a cyclical exchange of matter and energy between these two organelles within eukaryotic cells.",
